Changes.log
============

Session date: 2026-09-22
Files affected: dxpdb2mrc.c, dxpdb2mrc-bfactor.c
(Both files received identical changes.)


1. Fixed ringing in the resolution filter
------------------------------------------------------------

Fix:
  Modified soft-edged raised-cosine (Hann-style)
  taper centered on the cutoff frequency:
    - weight = 1.0                                            for fmag <= f1
    - weight = 0.5*(1 + cos(pi*(fmag-f1)/(f2-f1)))             for f1 < fmag < f2
    - weight = 0.0                                             for fmag >= f2
  where f1 = cutoff_freq - edge_width/2, f2 = cutoff_freq + edge_width/2.
  Fourier coefficients are multiplied by this weight instead of being
  zeroed outright.

New CLI option:
  -res_edge <width>   Taper width in 1/Å, centered on the resolution cutoff.
                       If not given, defaults to 0.2/resolution (i.e. a
                       taper spanning 20% of the cutoff frequency).

Other related edits:
  - Added an #ifndef M_PI guard (some platforms/standards don't define it
    in math.h) near the top of each file.
  - apply_resolution_filter() signature gained an edge_width parameter.
  - Usage string and main() updated to parse/pass -res_edge.


2. Added mask generation around the atomic model
------------------------------------------------------------

New feature: the program can now additionally compute and write a soft mask
on the same grid (box size, pixel size, origin) as the density map.

Algorithm (all new functions, added just before the Peng kernel section):

  a) build_atom_mask()
     For every atom, marks mask[voxel] = 1 for all voxels within
     -mask_radius Å of the atom center (hard sphere test), using the same
     walk-splat traversal pattern as the density renderer pDenRenderPeng.
     This is the binary "core" mask.

  b) Optional pre-filter (only if -mask_smooth > 0):
     gaussian_smooth_fft() Gaussian-blurs the binary core mask in Fourier
     space (multiply by exp(-2*pi^2*sigma^2*f^2), sigma = -mask_smooth in Å;
     a Gaussian filter has no sharp edge in Fourier space, so it does not
     introduce ringing), then threshold_binary() re-binarizes the result at
     0.5. This rounds off the voxel-staircase edges left by the discrete
     atom-sphere splat in step (a) before cushion/edge are added.

  c) distance_transform_3d()
     Computes the exact Euclidean distance (in voxel units) from every grid
     point to the nearest core-mask voxel, using a separable O(N) squared
     distance transform (Felzenszwalb & Huttenlocher algorithm: three 1D
     passes, one per axis, via the internal dt_1d() helper). This gives a
     true geometric distance field rather than a per-atom approximation.

  d) mask_from_distance()
     Converts the distance field into the final soft mask using the same
     raised-cosine taper shape as the resolution filter, but applied in
     real (distance) space instead of Fourier space:
       - mask = 1.0                                   for dist <= cushion
       - mask = 0.5*(1+cos(pi*(dist-cushion)/edge))    for cushion < dist < cushion+edge
       - mask = 0.0                                    for dist >= cushion+edge

New CLI options:
  -mask_out <file.mrc>   Enables mask computation/output; if omitted, no
                          mask is built (feature is fully opt-in).
  -mask_radius <Å>       Core radius around each atom. Default 1.5.
  -mask_cushion <Å>      Extra distance beyond the core kept at full
                          weight (1.0). Default 3.0.
  -mask_edge <Å>         Raised-cosine taper width from 1 to 0, applied
                          beyond the cushion. Default 5.0.
  -mask_smooth <Å>       Gaussian sigma for the optional pre-filter step
                          described in (b) above. Default 0.0 (disabled).

Output:
  If -mask_out is given, the mask is written as its own MRC file (mode 2,
  float) using the same header geometry (box, pixel size, origin) as the
  density map, with its own amin/amax/amean computed from the mask data.

